 0.034332275390625, -0.00853729248046875, 0.02825927734375, 0.01611328125, 0.01006317138671875, -0.02166748046875, -0.01525115966796875, -0.01727294921875, 0.016571044921875, -0.021209716796875, -0.004726409912109375, 0.039703369140625, 0.01959228515625, -0.00446319580078125, 0.01294708251953125, 0.00327301025390625, 0.0063629150390625, 0.016387939453125, -0.0226287841796875, -0.01100921630859375, 0.035614013671875, 0.0117950439453125, 0.018035888671875, 0.0033435821533203125, 0.00042319297790527344, -0.0209503173828125, 0.004791259765625, 0.022674560546875, -7.861852645874023e-05, -0.0002865791320800781, 0.0228118896484375, -0.00902557373046875, 0.0173797607421875, -0.01360321044921875, 0.0010805130004882812, -0.01110076904296875, 0.016265869140625, 0.0188140869140625, 0.02032470703125, -0.015625, 0.0249176025390625, 0.0243072509765625, 0.004032135009765625, 0.035858154296875, -0.0297393798828125, 0.0173187255859375, 0.02008056640625, -0.0450439453125, 0.00881195068359375, -0.018798828125, 0.016510009765625, 0.0116729736328125, -0.03509521484375, 0.0095672607421875, 0.0124359130859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are a central tool to achieve low-carbon heat supplies, but their efficient operation is challenged by increasingly heterogeneous, partially time-varying renewable sources, the integration of thermal storage, and the presence of meshed (cyclic) topologies. In cyclic networks with multiple suppliers and storage, operational optimization is complicated by nonlinear hydraulic and thermal interactions, inter-temporal coupling via storage, and the combinatorial complexity of flow directions in cycles. **Existing methods typically address only acyclic or linearized models, or cannot efficiently guarantee global optimality for realistic, multi-period, cyclic networks with nonlinearities and storage.** For example, in a network with two suppliers and a storage tank connected in a loop, determining the optimal flow and temperature profiles over time requires resolving nonlinear pressure drops, mixing at nodes, and storage dynamics, all while avoiding non-physical cyclic flows\u2014a challenge not addressed by standard linear or tree-based approaches. **To our knowledge, no prior work provides a global optimization framework for nonlinear, cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, along with scalable algorithmic enhancements and comprehensive evaluation on realistic benchmarks. This work fills that gap.**\n\n---\n\n**Main Contributions:**  \n- **Global Optimization Framework:** We develop a global optimization framework for cyclic district heating networks with multiple suppliers and storage, capturing nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints.\n- **Explicit Problem Formulation:** We provide a summary of the full nonlinear mixed-integer optimization problem, including variables, objective, and main constraints.\n- **Algorithmic Enhancements:** We propose five novel algorithmic enhancements to accelerate spatial branch-and-bound for these networks, each with explicit mathematical or algorithmic formulation and illustrative examples.\n- **Comprehensive Evaluation:** We provide a detailed evaluation on both generated and real-world benchmark instances, with explicit parameter ranges, statistical analysis, and confidence intervals for key metrics.\n- **Time Decomposition Analysis:** We analyze a time decomposition approach for multi-period operation with storage, rigorously discussing its assumptions, empirical validity, and limitations.\n- **Discussion and Future Work:** We discuss limitations in detail, provide concrete next steps, and outline plans for open-sourcing code and data, including current availability.\n\n---\n\n**Optimization Problem Formulation:**  \nLet \\( G = (V, E) \\) be the network graph. The main variables are:\n- \\( q_{e,t} \\): flow in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\)\n- \\( T_{v,t} \\): temperature at node \\( v \\) at time \\( t \\)\n- \\( s_{j,t} \\): storage state for storage unit \\( j \\) at time \\( t \\)\n- \\( x_{e,t} \\): binary variable for flow direction in pipe \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\)\n\n**Objective:**  \nMinimize total operational cost over all time periods, e.g.,\n\\[\n\\min \\sum_{t} \\sum_{i \\in \\text{suppliers}} c_i(q_{i,t}, T_{i,t}) + \\sum_{j \\in \\text{storage}} c_j(s_{j,t})\n\\]\nwhere \\( c_i \\) and \\( c_j \\) are cost functions for suppliers and storage.\n\n**Main Constraints:**  \n- **Nonlinear pressure drops:**  \n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{e,t} = k_e \\cdot q_{e,t}^2 \\quad \\forall e, t\n  \\]\n- **Temperature mixing at nodes:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{v,t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} T_{e,t}^{\\text{in}}}{\\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t}} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n- **Supply/return temperature bounds:**  \n  \\[\n  T_{i,t}^{\\min} \\leq T_{i,t} \\leq T_{i,t}^{\\max} \\quad \\forall i \\in \\text{suppliers}, t\n  \\]\n- **Mass and energy conservation:**  \n  \\[\n  \\sum_{e \\in \\text{in}(v)} q_{e,t} = \\sum_{e \\in \\text{out}(v)} q_{e,t} \\quad \\forall v, t\n  \\]\n- **Thermal storage dynamics:**  \n  \\[\n  s_{j,t+1} = s_{j,t} + \\eta_{\\text{charge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{charge}} - \\eta_{\\text{discharge}} q_{j,t}^{\\text{discharge}} \\quad \\forall j, t\n  \\]\n- **Cycle-breaking constraints:**  \n  (see below)\n- **Variable domains:**  \n  \\( q_{e,t} \\in \\mathbb{R} \\), \\( T_{v,t} \\in [T^{\\min}, T^{\\max}] \\), \\( s_{j,t} \\in [0, S_j^{\\max}] \\), \\( x_{e,t} \\in \\{0,1\\} \\)\n\n---\n\n**Algorithmic Enhancements:**  \n\n1. **Cycle-Breaking Constraints:**  \n   To prevent non-physical cyclic flows (flows circulating endlessly in network cycles without serving demand), we add, for each fundamental cycle \\( C \\), the constraint:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{e \\in C} x_{e,t} \\leq |C| - 1\n   \\]\n   where \\( x_{e,t} \\) indicates the direction of flow in edge \\( e \\) at time \\( t \\). *Example:* In a 3-edge cycle, at most 2 edges can have flow in the same direction, preventing a closed loop.\n\n2. **Supply/Return Symmetry Reduction:**  \n   Many networks exhibit symmetry between supply and return paths. We identify automorphisms in the network graph and add symmetry-breaking constraints, such as:\n   \\[\n   q_{i_1,t} \\geq q_{i_2,t} \\geq \\ldots \\geq q_{i_k,t}\n   \\]\n   for suppliers \\( i_1, i_2, \\ldots, i_k \\), enforcing a lexicographic order. This reduces redundant search over equivalent solutions.\n\n3. **Aggregated Temperature Mixing Constraints:**  \n   For nodes where multiple consumers are connected at a single junction, we aggregate their mixing constraints:\n   \\[\n   T_{\\text{agg},t}^{\\text{out}} = \\frac{\\sum_{i} q_{i,t} T_{i,t}}{\\sum_{i} q_{i,t}}\n   \\]\n   where \\( i \\) indexes the aggregated consumers. *Example:* If three consumers at a node each receive 2, 3, and 5 units of flow at 60\u00b0C, 65\u00b0C, and 70\u00b0C, the outgoing temperature is \\( (2 \\times 60 + 3 \\times 65 + 5 \\times 70)/10 = 66.5^\\circ \\)C.\n\n4. **Primal Heuristics:**  \n   We implement a greedy heuristic that, for each consumer node, assigns flow from the nearest available supplier (in terms of network distance), subject to pressure and temperature bounds. The heuristic proceeds as follows:\n   - Initialize all storage changes to zero.\n   - For each consumer, select the shortest path to a supplier with available capacity.\n   - Assign flow along this path to meet demand, update supplier and pipe capacities.\n   - Repeat for all consumers.\n   This provides a feasible starting point for the global solver.\n\n5. **Tailored Branching Rules:**  \n   During branch-and-bound, we prioritize branching on variables with the highest impact on feasibility, such as:\n   - Flows on critical pipes (identified via betweenness centrality).\n   - Storage state variables at time steps with largest dual infeasibility.\n   For example, if the relaxed solution has a flow variable \\( q_{e,t} \\) with high fractional value and high dual infeasibility, it is branched first.\n\nFurther technical details and pseudocode for each enhancement are provided in the supplementary material.\n\n---\n\n**Experimental Evaluation:**  \n\n**Benchmark Instances:**  \n- **Generated Instances:** 40 networks with 10\u201350 nodes, 2\u20135 suppliers, 1\u20133 storage units, and 1\u20133 fundamental cycles. Topologies are generated using random geometric graphs with added cycles.  \n- **Real-World Instances:** 6 anonymized networks from a European utility, with 12\u201330 nodes, 1\u20133 suppliers, and 1\u20132 storage units.  \n- **Parameter Ranges:**  \n  | Parameter                | Range / Value         |\n  |--------------------------|----------------------|\n  | Pipe roughness (k)       | 0.02\u20130.08            |\n  | Supply temp. bounds (\u00b0C) | 70\u2013120               |\n  | Return temp. bounds (\u00b0C) | 40\u201370                |\n  | Storage capacity (MWh)   | 5\u201350                 |\n  | Storage efficiency (%)   | 90\u201398                |\n\n**Experimental Setup:**  \nAll experiments were run on an Intel Xeon 2.6 GHz CPU with 64 GB RAM, using BARON 22.1.1 as the global solver and default tolerances unless otherwise specified.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics and Results:**  \n- **Solution Rate:** Number of instances solved to global optimality within 1800 seconds.\n- **Runtime:** Median and interquartile range (IQR) of solution times.\n- **Optimality Gap:** Final relative gap (%).\n- **Confidence Intervals:** 95% confidence intervals (CI) for solution rates and runtimes.\n- **Statistical Analysis:** Fisher\u2019s exact test for solution rates; Wilcoxon signed"}}